            <ArticleTitle>Injection of alcohol into bone metastases under CT guidance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>932-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Percutaneous injection of ethanol was evaluated in terminally ill cancer patients with painful osteolytic bone metastases treated previously without success by radiation or chemotherapy and conventional pain therapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND MATERIALS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-five patients with 27 bone metastases in various locations underwent one to three instillations of 3-25 ml ethanol (95%) under CT guidance, which allowed precise needle positioning and control of the diffusion of alcohol thus minimizing the complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Within 24 to 48 h 74% of the cases experienced a reduction of analgesic needs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Percutaneous ethanol injection under CT guidance is useful in reducing pain and in improving the quality of life of patients with advanced stages of cancer and painful bone metastases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
